Subject: Routing problem on DEC/OSF1 hosts
Message-ID: <1994Apr28.152923.23728@dxcern.cern.ch>
Summary: ICMP-Redirect information not used on Alpha OSF1 hosts.
Keywords: Dynamic routing
Reply-To: dietrich@dxcern.cern.ch
Organization: CERN European Lab for Particle Physics
Date: Thu, 28 Apr 1994 15:29:23 GMT
Lines: 56




One of our users discovered a routing problem in apparently all
current versions of Digital OSF1.

If a host is started up with only a default gateway and no other
routing information, an attempt to connect to a host on a different
local network will result in the creation of dynamically generated
routing information in the routing tables of the system (ICMP Redirect).

However it seems that this information is ignored under OSF1 in all
subsequent attempts to connect to the original target host.  Replacement
of the dynamically generated information, flagged in the "netstat -r"
display with "UGD" by static information using "route delete" and "route
add" with the appropriate parameters solves the problem, however in an
unsatisfactory way.

We are running neither routed nor gated on our machines.

ULTRIX behaves correctly, a UGD entry in the routing table is
properly used.

Example:

Host afcern.cern.ch (128.141.201.213) on network ether (128.141) starts
after being booted with the following 'netstat -r' result (Forget about
the usage counts, I took the output a long time after the boot):

Routing tables
Destination      Gateway            Flags     Refs     Use  Interface
Netmasks:
Inet             255.255.0.0

Route Tree for Protocol Family 2:
default          cgate1             UG          3  1894834  fta0
localhost        localhost          UH          1      414  lo0
ether            afcern             U          50  5275463  fta0

After an attempt to telnet to host oxford.cern.ch (192.16.160.42)
there is an additional line:

192.16.160       pcrlcn-gw          UGD         0        0  fta0

which is the dynamically generated routing information, but the
telnet command will eventually time out!

This is a rather unfortunate behaviour, and, even worse, Release
2.0 IS NO BETTER.

I would appreciate your comments.

Dietrich Wiegandt
CERN Computing and Networks Division
CH-1211 Geneve 23
Switzerland


