ronm@SLCSRV.SLAC.STANFORD.EDU writes:

There's a book "UNIX FOR VMS USERS" available from DEC press.  It's also
at Stacy's in Palo Alto and Technical Books in Sunnyvale.

The project you outline is non-trivial (no sh*t!) and contains several
parts that don't translate well from VMS to UNIX.

Off the top of my head here are my thougts:

1) most unix systems can and don't do REALTIME.  They aren't designed
to respond this way.  Finding a system that does sort of obiates the
port because you're porting to allow moving to other hardware and away
from DEC, right?

2) VAX FORTRAN isn't really transportable to other platforms (unless
there are 3rd party compilers), especially if you're using the 
non-ANSI stuff like RECORD rather than EQUIVALENCEd COMMONs.

3) VAX C issues can be examined now by getting the GNU C compiler and
running your stuff through that.  If it works OK with that, you're
in good shape as the GNU C is on lots of systems and it's the same
on all of them.

4) There's no such thing as Sharable images or global sections
on Unix.  That part has to go.  Use sockets or pipes to
communicate to different processes and a communications
protocol.

5) Port the MACRO to C.

6) Pay no attention to POSIX on the VAX.  You can use your system
to prototype and get it running on your existing HW until you buy
the UNIX you want, but the REALTIME stuff is still up in the air.

7) CMS and DTM issues: use the UNIX tools and through out the DEC
ones.  There's no programming interface to SCCS or RCS unless you
by commercial one or use some GNU utility to "roll your own".

8) Network performance (with the use of sockets) would allow lots
of stuff to be run on different systems.  Until you have a functional
spec and design spec, it's way to early to talk performance (wait
at least until V1.0 is running before looking at it).

9) I don't know about UNIX drivers, but the people who can write
them are expensive (as are the VMS people).

10) How to do development while running production w/o 2 concurrent
systems?  You go me on this one.  Try alternate time lines.  I don't
see a way to do UNIX development on VMS production system.  IMHO,
development and production environments MUST be kept separate or
there will be turf wars and political issues.

This port is a substantial project that will take at least 1-2
man years to complete (just my seat of the pants guestimate, but
I'm sure you've gone through the functional spec [which I hope
you have one, otherwise that's the first step] and tasked it
out).  At DEC, I'd occationally be asked to guestimate on such
projects as business.  Unfortunately, most computer vendors will
spec a project low to get the business and end up taking a bath.

You might try taking this to DEC as a project and see what they
think it would cost to port.  Other firms might give you different
estimates.  One thing I've learned about such projects is that
the more opinions you get, the more diverse they are and the more
unsure you are of the project.  But, the more people look at it,
the better idea you get on what it would take.

Good luck, you'll need lots of it!

/MeV/
