In article <2pio4e$958@cisun2000.unil.ch>, khofmann@isrec-sun1.unil.ch (Kay Hofmann) writes...
>Is there a way in DECpascal (or, possibly in all DEC languages for OSF1) to
>access the environment variables of unix? I didn't find nay hints in the
>language reference and user manuals.
> 
You can use the getenv(3) routine from libc.  Since Pascal's strings
aren't nul-terminated, you need to do some extra work to deal with
them.

Here's a working example:

program getenv(input,output);

type str255 = packed array [1..255] of char;
     strptr = ^str255;

var  envname  : string(255);
     envvalue : strptr;

function getenv( name : str255 ) : strptr; external;

begin
write('Environment variable to convert? ');
readln(envname);
envvalue := getenv(envname + chr(0));
writeln('Value is ',substr(envvalue^,1,index(envvalue^,chr(0))));
end.

