HelloBased on recent experience with the aforementioned tape drive,
I'd like to verify some facts or assumptions.  The drive I'm using the
most is hooked to an Alpha with OSF 1.3 on it.

1) Using /dev/rmt0a means that COMPACTION WILL NOT BE USED when
writing to the tape.  Source: "man tz" on OSF

2) Using /dev/rmt0h means that, REGARDLESS of whether the input files
are compressed, COMPACTION IS TURNED ON.  (Source: "man tz" again,
emphasis mine)

3) Using "tar cf /dev/rmt0h" with files that are already compressed
(ie, with gzip or compress, or a compressed image format like GIF or
JPEG) will cause the archive to use MORE tape than if the files were
not compressed. 

Source: experience!  I tried to archive 300 meg of compressed stuff
and I could only get 200 meg or so onto a single tape.  Since these
tapes are 90 meters and are supposed to hold about 2 gigabytes,
something was wrong.  When I tried again using "dd of=/dev/rmt0a", all
was fine.

4) The TLZ06 can do mixed uncompacted/compacted archives on the same
tape!  Thus, if you archive files with /dev/rmt0a, and then later
append to that archive with /dev/rmt0h, the tape drive obliges,
subjecting you to the same pitfall in (3).  Source: experience again

----------------- END INCLUDED TEXT ---------------

Additional comments:

1) I can use "dd" to start an archive, but I like using "tar rf" to
APPEND to the archive because I don't like dealing with multiple
archives on the same tape.  Unfortunately, I don't know of a way to
use "dd" to append to an existing archive.  But when I use "tar rf", I
run out of tape.

2) These tape operations take a LONG TIME!  Here's one thing I tried:
   a) Create a new tape archive: "tar cf /dev/rmt0a" (tape at
      beginning) I put about 200 meg on it, no problem.
   b) Append another  meg with "tar rf /dev/rmt0a".  It reached the
      end of the media overnight; next morning I finished it off with
      a new tape.  STILL did not get 1.9 gigabytes onto a single tape.
      It took 8 hours to get a "tar tvf" of the tape!

3) It seems to take a super long time only after the second operation.
If I make a new archive: "tar cf /dev/rmt0a", I can get a listing with
"tar tvf" in a normal (for tapes) amount of time.  After appending to
the archive, it takes forever.


Anyway, those are some of my trials and tribulations.  Anyone else
having these weird problems?  Perhaps not!  Please clue us in to the
secrets.

