august1@server.uwindsor.ca (Augustyn  Robert) writes:

>I have some problems with login to the system,
>if I type my login in Capitol letters the sesion gets
>out of control, I get something like this:
>DEC OSF/1 Version 1.3 (integram) tty0G

>login: ROBERT

>\PASSWORD:

>\LOGIN INCORRECT

>LOGIN: ROBERT

> and everething is in capital, is there a way to fix it:
>Thanks in advance for the help.
>robert

If the first letter entered on the login prompt is a capital
one, the system assumes that your terminal is only capable
of capital letters, and so sends all output  in capitals.

This has been the case since the Dark Ages of Unix(tm) when
this kind of brain damaged terminal was more abundant than it 
is now.

Solution: don't use capitals as login names. And when you
want to 'reset' things, enter a ^D at the login: prompt.

Hope this helps

Wilko

