You can do this kind of thing, but you have to be VERY careful.  A lot
of these kinds of function definitions in old code are based on the 
very non-portable assumption that all the arguments to the function, and
all the return values that can be returned, are the same size.  This is 
NOT true on DEC OSF/1 - longs and pointers are twice the size of integers.

The correct way to do this sort of thing is with varargs.  Barring that,
code the function so that all the args and the return value are some
generic type like (void *), then be very careful to cast the args inside
the function before use.  Similarly cast the return value from the function
to the correct type on return.

I know this sounds like a hassle, but the code will be more portable after
it is done.  Even under K&R, the only assumption that could be made was
that sizeof(int) <= sizeof(long), and there was no way at all to relate to
size of pointer types.  A lot of code made those assumptions, but it is 
inherently non-portable.

