(Sebastian Meyer) writes:
>I get this message from ld when linking some 50000 lines of Fortran:
>
>f77 -O -o xnjoy njoy.o reconr.o [...20 more modules...]
>ld:
>loc_: common but defined as text in a shared object
>

There is an external routine named loc in one of the shared libraries;
this got ported over from RISC ULTRIX and is not documented; it basically
does the same as %LOC (but unless you declare it to be INTEGER*8 it
returns only the low-order 32 bits. 

I believe the message is telling you that you have a COMMON block that is
also named loc; generally on UNIX systems you can't have a COMMON block
and an external routine by the same name.  (The Fortran standard also
says you can't do this, though it works fine on OpenVMS.)

If you're not using loc as a routine name, I would recommend ignoring
the message as it's harmless.  DEC Fortran V3.4 (I think - can check if
anyone cares - send me mail) has added support for loc as an intrinsic;
basically an alias for %LOC and not using an external routine.

>BTW: Is there a usable debugger for Fortran on the Alpha? dbx is
>     completely lost and ladbx doesn't seem to support Fortran. If
>     I only had beloved gdb ....

We've been doing a LOT of work on dbx; you should install DEC OSF/1 V2.0
and see if it improves in the areas you're interested in.  I think there
are further updates to dbx available as well through Digital software
support.  We're also working on adding full Fortran support to ladbx,
but it's not there yet.

