>In article <2nuhns$qb2@senator-bedfellow.MIT.EDU>, tuyo@mumford (Mike M. Tuyo) writes:
>|> I'm trying to port a *huge* program written for a 32-bit machine
>|> to the alpha, and my resident compiler is gcc (2.5.7).  It seems
>|> to have quite a bit of problems with the longs being 8-bytes wide.
>|> 
>|> Can someone tell my what options to give gcc so it makes longs 4-bytes
>|> wide instead of 8?
>|> 
>|> Thank you
>|> -mike tuyo.

If you can use the native compiler, try the -taso option to cc and ld.
It stands for Truncated Address Support Option, and it makes the code
run using 32 bit addresses. I believe longs are still 8 bytes, but addresses
only use the first 4 bytes.

You have to have chosen to install it on your system since it's not
installed automatically.  I haven't tried it on my system since we
didn't install it, but I hear it works OK.

Ken


