In article <Co48IK.8KK@unix.portal.com>, sdr@shell.portal.com (Stephen Reagan) writes:
> clusters are syncing on the same master; we need to have the various groups
> of machines to each have a separate master time server without severing the
> network.  Is this possible???

Use NTP. It is a dramatic improvement over timed, and implements a hierarchy
of servers as opposed to the timed single (voted) master. It also understands
that some clocks are better than others, and can deal with network
partitioning. At one point, I hacked a timed to ignore the "time vote" protocol
so that I could run a few timed-only systems on an primarily-NTP network, and
even that had problems keeping good time. NTP version 2 (or maybe even better)
is available somewhere on the net.


