In article <2oh2ke$g1l@pravda.sdsc.edu> steube@elroy.sdsc.edu (Ken Steube) writes:
>If you can use the native compiler, try the -taso option to cc and ld.
>It stands for Truncated Address Support Option, and it makes the code
>run using 32 bit addresses. I believe longs are still 8 bytes, but addresses
>only use the first 4 bytes.

Not quite. It arranges for code and data to be laid out in memory so
that the *value* of the addresses fits into 32 bits, i.e. they survive
the following:

	int i;
        char *p;

	p = "foo";
	i = (int) p;
        p = (char *) i;

There is no way of getting either 32 bit addresses or 32 bit longs. Fix
the program (which isn't too hard with the help of lint -q). At least
we did it here for the ISODE and multimedia mail system, which has several
hundred thousand lines of code.

