In article <2lt591$c18@utne.zk3.dec.com> ddhill@mytoy.zk3.dec.com (Dave Hill WhatGE) writes:
>In article <2lqnbi$hts@krishna.cs.umd.edu>, harry@cs.umd.edu (Harry Mantakos) writes:
>|> Do I have any reason to be optimistic about accelerated graphics
>|> support for DEC hardware in R6?
>|> -harry
>
>The real question is how much of that hardware support will be folded back
>to the XC in time for R6.
>
>At a minimum, I think that non-accelerated HX code will be fed back. This
>is probably similar to what you are seeing with the MX & HX under R5 and
>Ultrix, basically just a dumb color frame buffer support.

Well, this means a great deal to us. We have a couple hundred 
Suns and DECstations, and we're considering moving into Alpha's.
We have always favored the mit/XC distribution over the vendor's
OpenWindows or DECwindows for a few reasons, for instance:

	- we have source code to it, and thus can make our own changes 
	  and apply fixes. We do OS research, and sometimes we can use 
	  the XC code when using the vendor's server binaries isn't
	  an option.

	- the vendor's code is typically a release or two behind the
	  X consortium release. For instance, Ultrix was, until recently, 
	  still shipping  with R3-era libraries and clients and an R4-era 
	  server. This can mean losing out on "new" features; MAGIC-COOKIE 
	  support came along with mit R4, font servers in R5, and R6 
	  is purported to include Kerberos authentication. These all came
	  years before the vendor's servers had them.

	- when you're in a heterogeneous environment you start to 
	  appreciate the ability to use the same "thing" across all your 
	  platforms as much as possible. Using vanilla mit server and 
	  clients helps prevent the weird incompatibilities we've seen 
	  between various parts of the vendor supplied distributions 
	  (mit client/xnews server, OpenWindows clients/DECwindows 
	  server, etc.) These incompatibilities shouldn't happen, but 
	  nonetheless they sometimes do.

The chances are good that we'll want to use the XC sample server on any
machine we bring in here. Given that, if the support that DEC contributes
to the XC is just at the level of a dumb frame buffer, then their product
is not as useful to me as, say, a SPARCstation, given that Sun has 
contributed (albeit in a object-only form) some accelerated support for 
their GX graphics. The net result for us is that the SPARC has good 
graphics performance and the Alpha or DECstation makes me sit there and 
watch the text in my xterms scroll.

Nobody is going to claim that DEC is obligated legally or morally to do 
this, but the customers need this code and since the interfaces are 
proprietary, only DEC can decide to provide it. And since it makes their 
workstations that much more useful (and thus, more buyable :-), it seems 
to me to be in DEC's best interest.
-harry
