>>I really don't understand DEC's problem.  Their hardware is wonderful,
>>their OS is wonderful, but they're shooting themselves in the foot by
>>not supporting third party software developers.  They really need to
>>go the extra mile because their market share is so small.  People are
>>going to port things to Solaris no matter what, because the market is
>>so large.  DEC should go out and actively encourage software vendors
>>to port to the Alpha OSF/1 platform.  Every machine they lend out or
>>give away will probably give them 10 or 20 sales in return.


I personally wrote a proposal for an ISV a while back in which Digital 
offered nearly a million dollars in free equipment loans, free service,
and three full time consultants for a year to get a product ported.  The
ISV declined, citing support costs as the reason.  This ISV then told a 
customer interested in the product on an ALPHA platform that Digital was
not actively pursuing the port.  Why would the ISV do this?  Well, it is
believable to the customer, and it beats saying "we're too cheap to port
to this new, fast machine when we can arm twist you into buying inferior
hardware - and still get our license fee with no further investment".

That's why I was saying a while back that customers need to understand the
economics of the software market.  The software vendors don't care a bit
about which platform is technically better.  If a software vendor believes
they'll get the sale no matter what, then they have no incentive to port.
That's why one of the criteria for application selection becomes "does it 
run on the platforms that I want?".  If you, as customers, don't make it 
clear to software vendors that supporting specific platforms is part of your
selection process, the vendors will support the minimum selection of hardware
that they can get away with. It's a simple matter of cost/benefit analysis.

Letting your software vendor dictate your choice of hardware is NOT a good
idea.  In the final analysis, is it any better to get locked into a hardware
platform by a proprietary application than by a proprietary OS?  You lose 
freedom of choice in any regard.  If there's a package you want on Alpha, and
the software vendor says, "well, you have to buy brand X to get the software",
buying that other hardware platform sends a message to the software company
that says that you don't care about the hardware platform, and that the
software company doesn't need to invest in a port.  It ain't got a thing to
do with Digital.  Tell the vendor that you'll have to evaluate packages that
DO run on Alpha, though, and they just might get more negotiable.

