In article <2n9tsd$e4i@netnews.upenn.edu>, david@udcemail.udc.upenn.edu (R. David Murray) writes:
|>
|>One of my users sent mail to "anyone" and it landed in the mailbox of
|>a student who had set their full name to "Holmes anyone?"  The presence
|>of the "?" in the fullname seems to be the key.
|>
|>Why?  Is this a standrard sendmail feature?  Can I turn it off?
|>

It doesn't have much to do with the questionmark character, sendmail,
when delivering mail to a name, does an educated guess on all available
information - aliases, usernames AND 'gcos'-Fullnames. If a match is
unique, sendmail will use it. If the user in question MUST have a fullname
which contains 'anyone' mail sent to 'anyone' will reach him. I would
see no reason why one may switch this behavior off - force your users
to have correct fullnames instead.

BTW, my email address (see below) works BECAUSE sendmail does it this way.

